WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:02.260
- Tell me Tommy,

00:00:02.260 --> 00:00:05.190
why are you so sure this is Santa Claus?

00:00:05.190 --> 00:00:08.386
- Because my daddy told me so.
(courtroom laughing)

00:00:08.386 --> 00:00:09.300
Didn't you daddy?

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:10.854
- Hearsay.

00:00:10.854 --> 00:00:13.437
(upbeat music)

00:00:14.625 --> 00:00:15.458
Hey legal eagles,

00:00:15.458 --> 00:00:16.960
it's time to think like a lawyer,

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:19.890
and it's time to save Santa Claus.

00:00:19.890 --> 00:00:22.170
Today we are covering
the Christmas classic

00:00:22.170 --> 00:00:23.790
Miracle on 34th Street.

00:00:23.790 --> 00:00:26.160
Now, I may be a jaded, cynical lawyer,

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:28.360
but I love a good holiday movie,

00:00:28.360 --> 00:00:31.350
especially one that involves
lawyers saving the day.

00:00:31.350 --> 00:00:33.468
Because who is is going
to save Santa Claus

00:00:33.468 --> 00:00:35.780
if not for the lawyers?

00:00:35.780 --> 00:00:38.960
(bells chiming)

00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:39.840
If you disagree with me,

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:42.070
be sure to comment in
the form of an objection,

00:00:42.070 --> 00:00:44.410
which I'll either sustain or overrule.

00:00:44.410 --> 00:00:46.190
And I will pin the best comment

00:00:46.190 --> 00:00:48.410
that most thinks like a lawyer.

00:00:48.410 --> 00:00:50.241
Of course, stay until the end,

00:00:50.241 --> 00:00:51.074
when I give Miracle on 34th Street

00:00:51.074 --> 00:00:52.500
a grade for legal realism,

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:55.170
and we settle once and
for all the question

00:00:55.170 --> 00:00:59.170
of whether there really
is a Santa Claus or not.

00:00:59.170 --> 00:01:00.540
So, without further adieu,

00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:04.512
let's dig in to the original
Miracle on 24th Street.

00:01:04.512 --> 00:01:08.262
(band playing holiday music)

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:20.063
Terrible Santa Claus.

00:01:25.210 --> 00:01:28.350
Oh, could be public intoxication.

00:01:28.350 --> 00:01:29.470
Big trouble.

00:01:29.470 --> 00:01:30.870
- She's a little confused,

00:01:30.870 --> 00:01:32.910
and I thought maybe you could
help to straighten her out.

00:01:32.910 --> 00:01:33.940
- I'd be glad to.

00:01:33.940 --> 00:01:35.597
- Would you please tell her

00:01:35.597 --> 00:01:37.830
that you're not really Santa Claus?

00:01:37.830 --> 00:01:40.343
That there actually is no such person.

00:01:40.343 --> 00:01:41.440
- Ugh, how dare she!

00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:43.413
- I disagree with you Mrs. Walker,

00:01:43.413 --> 00:01:44.623
but not only is there such a person,

00:01:44.623 --> 00:01:45.456
but here I am to prove it.

00:01:45.456 --> 00:01:47.400
- No, no, no, you misunderstand.

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:48.936
I want you to tell her the truth.

00:01:48.936 --> 00:01:50.590
What's your name?

00:01:50.590 --> 00:01:51.423
- Kris Kringle.

00:01:51.423 --> 00:01:52.479
I'll bet you're in the first grade.

00:01:52.479 --> 00:01:53.723
- Second.

00:01:53.723 --> 00:01:55.450
- I mean, your real name.

00:01:55.450 --> 00:01:56.940
- That is my real name.

00:01:56.940 --> 00:01:59.040
- So there is a
philosophical discussion here

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:00.360
that this movie sort of skirts,

00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:04.340
which is, what does it
actually mean to be someone.

00:02:04.340 --> 00:02:07.330
What if your particular subjective belief

00:02:07.330 --> 00:02:08.300
about your own identity

00:02:08.300 --> 00:02:10.670
doesn't comport with what
everybody else thinks.

00:02:10.670 --> 00:02:13.040
Is there such a thing as a platonic ideal

00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:14.650
of someone's identity?

00:02:14.650 --> 00:02:16.560
These are some heady,
philosophical questions

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:18.110
that I'm not even going to try to answer.

00:02:18.110 --> 00:02:20.281
But I think we should
probably keep that in mind

00:02:20.281 --> 00:02:25.250
as we delve into a trial over
one's own personal identity.

00:02:25.250 --> 00:02:26.200
- Now,

00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:27.680
I want you to stand
with your feet together

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:28.513
and your arms extended.

00:02:28.513 --> 00:02:30.747
- Then I want you to
muscular coordination test.

00:02:30.747 --> 00:02:32.490
Surely.

00:02:32.490 --> 00:02:33.640
Be glad to (chuckling).

00:02:37.390 --> 00:02:40.660
You know, sometimes the cause
of nervous habits like yours

00:02:40.660 --> 00:02:41.900
is not obvious.

00:02:41.900 --> 00:02:45.526
No, often they're the
result of an insecurity.

00:02:45.526 --> 00:02:47.400
Are you happy at home Mr. Sawyer?

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:48.630
- That will be all Mr. Kringle!

00:02:48.630 --> 00:02:51.241
The examination's over, you may go.

00:02:51.241 --> 00:02:52.870
- I have to believe

00:02:52.870 --> 00:02:55.790
that having an on-staff
psychologist or therapist

00:02:55.790 --> 00:02:58.960
is pretty unusual in a department store.

00:02:58.960 --> 00:02:59.980
Even in the 1940s,

00:02:59.980 --> 00:03:03.070
it seems kind of weird to have
someone permanently on staff

00:03:03.070 --> 00:03:03.940
who does nothing

00:03:03.940 --> 00:03:06.110
but psychologically
evaluate your employees.

00:03:06.110 --> 00:03:08.200
You're probably doing something wrong,

00:03:08.200 --> 00:03:09.930
creating a dangerous workplace,

00:03:09.930 --> 00:03:12.870
if you have to have a psychologist on hand

00:03:12.870 --> 00:03:14.260
in your department store.

00:03:14.260 --> 00:03:15.650
It seems a little weird.

00:03:15.650 --> 00:03:17.115
- I beg your pardon!

00:03:17.115 --> 00:03:18.040
- You're job here, I understand,

00:03:18.040 --> 00:03:20.320
is to give intelligence tests.

00:03:20.320 --> 00:03:22.700
Passing yourself off as a psychologist.

00:03:22.700 --> 00:03:23.970
You ought to be horsewhipped.

00:03:23.970 --> 00:03:26.770
Taking a normal,
impressionable boy like Alfred,

00:03:26.770 --> 00:03:28.500
and filling him up with
complexes and phobias?

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:30.830
- I think I'm better equipped
to judge that than you are.

00:03:30.830 --> 00:03:31.730
- Just because the boy

00:03:31.730 --> 00:03:33.070
wants to be good and kind to children,

00:03:33.070 --> 00:03:34.400
you tell him he has a guilt complex.

00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:35.370
- Having the same delusion,

00:03:35.370 --> 00:03:36.910
you couldn't possibly understand.

00:03:36.910 --> 00:03:38.450
The boy is definitely maladjusted,

00:03:38.450 --> 00:03:39.597
and I'm helping him.

00:03:39.597 --> 00:03:40.604
- Maladjusted?

00:03:40.604 --> 00:03:41.437
You talk about maladjusted.

00:03:41.437 --> 00:03:43.140
Seems to me that the patient
is running the clinic here.

00:03:43.140 --> 00:03:44.480
- I won't stand...

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:45.313
Leave this office immediately.

00:03:45.313 --> 00:03:46.870
- Now either you stop--

00:03:46.870 --> 00:03:47.703
- Oh, he might be
trespassing at this point.

00:03:47.703 --> 00:03:48.840
- Straight to Mr. Macy

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:50.841
and tell him what a malicious,

00:03:50.841 --> 00:03:51.841
contemptible fraud you are!

00:03:51.841 --> 00:03:52.950
- Get out before I'll have you thrown out!

00:03:52.950 --> 00:03:56.426
- There's only one way
to handle a man like you.

00:03:56.426 --> 00:03:58.280
You won't listen to reason.

00:03:58.280 --> 00:03:59.810
You're heartless.

00:03:59.810 --> 00:04:01.310
You have no humanity.

00:04:01.310 --> 00:04:02.335
- Are you going to leave?

00:04:02.335 --> 00:04:04.534
- Yes.

00:04:04.534 --> 00:04:06.110
(umbrella smacking forehead)
- Oh!

00:04:06.110 --> 00:04:07.163
- Oh boy!

00:04:08.020 --> 00:04:09.760
I thought if you were naughty
you got a lump of coal

00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:11.111
in your stocking,

00:04:11.111 --> 00:04:12.520
not bashed in the head with a cudgel.

00:04:12.520 --> 00:04:14.240
I think Santa might be
in some trouble here,

00:04:14.240 --> 00:04:17.610
because he has committed a
clear-cut case of battery.

00:04:17.610 --> 00:04:20.750
Battery is a simple tort
that only has two elements.

00:04:20.750 --> 00:04:21.583
Number one,

00:04:21.583 --> 00:04:22.712
you have to have intentional contact

00:04:22.712 --> 00:04:24.570
with a plaintiff's body.

00:04:24.570 --> 00:04:25.590
And number two,

00:04:25.590 --> 00:04:28.780
that intentional contact must
produce some sort of injury.

00:04:28.780 --> 00:04:30.770
But in New York, where
this movie takes place,

00:04:30.770 --> 00:04:32.910
there are several
different kinds of battery,

00:04:32.910 --> 00:04:34.810
including aggravated battery.

00:04:34.810 --> 00:04:38.110
So, to figure out which
particular variety of battery

00:04:38.110 --> 00:04:39.581
Kris Kringle has committed here,

00:04:39.581 --> 00:04:42.442
we need to think like a lawyer.

00:04:42.442 --> 00:04:45.025
(upbeat tones)

00:04:46.250 --> 00:04:48.270
New York has several different varieties

00:04:48.270 --> 00:04:50.170
of assault and battery,

00:04:50.170 --> 00:04:52.210
the first of which is the least severe,

00:04:52.210 --> 00:04:53.880
which is assault in the third degree,

00:04:53.880 --> 00:04:56.280
or commonly called simple assault,

00:04:56.280 --> 00:04:59.530
which is causing someone harm
with the intent to do so.

00:04:59.530 --> 00:05:01.840
It doesn't matter how
severe the injury is,

00:05:01.840 --> 00:05:04.400
so long as you have caused pain,

00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:07.320
you have made physical
contact with another person,

00:05:07.320 --> 00:05:08.860
without consent.

00:05:08.860 --> 00:05:10.640
That is a misdemeanor charge,

00:05:10.640 --> 00:05:13.560
which is punishable by
up to a year in jail.

00:05:13.560 --> 00:05:15.170
Now, the next type of assault

00:05:15.170 --> 00:05:16.960
is assault in the second degree,

00:05:16.960 --> 00:05:18.330
which generally you do by, again,

00:05:18.330 --> 00:05:20.300
making physical contact with someone,

00:05:20.300 --> 00:05:23.210
but with the intent to
do serious bodily harm,

00:05:23.210 --> 00:05:26.280
and actually committing
serious bodily harm;

00:05:26.280 --> 00:05:29.290
causing serious bodily harm in the victim.

00:05:29.290 --> 00:05:32.350
Not surprisingly, if you
commit second degree assault,

00:05:32.350 --> 00:05:34.190
because it is so much more severe,

00:05:34.190 --> 00:05:37.254
it carries with it a
much more severe penalty.

00:05:37.254 --> 00:05:40.200
Assault in the second
degree is a class D felony,

00:05:40.200 --> 00:05:43.160
and carries with it a
mandatory jail sentence.

00:05:43.160 --> 00:05:45.410
And then, finally, there is
assault in the first degree,

00:05:45.410 --> 00:05:48.270
which is the most severe form of assault,

00:05:48.270 --> 00:05:50.010
which is committed with the intent

00:05:50.912 --> 00:05:52.840
to disfigure or amputate the victim here.

00:05:52.840 --> 00:05:56.190
So it's a very, very serious
penalty that carries with it

00:05:56.190 --> 00:05:58.230
a mandatory, multi-year sentence.

00:05:58.230 --> 00:05:59.340
So it is a big deal.

00:05:59.340 --> 00:06:00.860
But let's put that aside for the moment,

00:06:00.860 --> 00:06:03.150
and just focus on the simple assault

00:06:03.150 --> 00:06:05.460
and potential second
degree assault charges

00:06:05.460 --> 00:06:07.920
against Santa Claus, Kris Kringle.

00:06:07.920 --> 00:06:09.870
Kris Kringle got into a heated argument

00:06:09.870 --> 00:06:11.550
with the fake psychiatrist.

00:06:11.550 --> 00:06:13.590
After basically yelling at each other,

00:06:13.590 --> 00:06:16.350
he picked up an umbrella, a hard object,

00:06:16.350 --> 00:06:19.230
and hit this psychiatrist
over the head with it.

00:06:19.230 --> 00:06:20.520
Under those circumstances,

00:06:20.520 --> 00:06:21.900
you might be able to conclude

00:06:21.900 --> 00:06:25.740
that he intended to
inflict serious bodily harm

00:06:25.740 --> 00:06:27.450
on the fake psychiatrist.

00:06:27.450 --> 00:06:28.790
On the other hand you might argue

00:06:28.790 --> 00:06:31.110
that since this is
ostensibly Santa Claus here,

00:06:31.110 --> 00:06:33.800
he did not intend to
seriously injure the man,

00:06:33.800 --> 00:06:36.090
but just to ring his bell

00:06:36.090 --> 00:06:38.320
and to cause him temporary suffering

00:06:38.320 --> 00:06:41.180
for what he has caused
the other little boy here.

00:06:41.180 --> 00:06:42.880
And you can see that the psychiatrist

00:06:42.880 --> 00:06:44.750
is not permanently harmed,

00:06:44.750 --> 00:06:47.610
and in fact malingers
and fakes the seriousness

00:06:47.610 --> 00:06:50.870
of his injury to get
sympathy from his coworkers.

00:06:50.870 --> 00:06:52.500
Which might militate in favor

00:06:52.500 --> 00:06:55.500
of it being a non-serious bodily injury,

00:06:55.500 --> 00:06:58.300
and really just being
unwanted physical contact,

00:06:58.300 --> 00:07:00.750
rather than the more severe penalties

00:07:00.750 --> 00:07:03.230
associated with assault
in the second degree.

00:07:03.230 --> 00:07:05.610
However, there is a complication here,

00:07:05.610 --> 00:07:08.540
because in New York, if you use a weapon,

00:07:08.540 --> 00:07:10.570
it is an automatic aggravation,

00:07:10.570 --> 00:07:14.220
from simple assault to
assault in the second degree.

00:07:14.220 --> 00:07:15.930
I think there is a reasonable argument

00:07:15.930 --> 00:07:19.330
that an umbrella, especially
the hard end of an umbrella,

00:07:19.330 --> 00:07:20.830
constitutes a weapon,

00:07:20.830 --> 00:07:22.760
and might take the simple assault

00:07:22.760 --> 00:07:24.730
from being unwanted physical conduct

00:07:24.730 --> 00:07:26.960
into the realm of second degree assault,

00:07:26.960 --> 00:07:28.980
which is a class D felony.

00:07:28.980 --> 00:07:30.530
So within the world of this movie,

00:07:30.530 --> 00:07:32.920
Kris Kringle has committed assault,

00:07:32.920 --> 00:07:36.350
and may be guilty of
felony aggravated assault

00:07:36.350 --> 00:07:38.730
in bopping the psychiatrist on the head

00:07:38.730 --> 00:07:40.056
with an umbrella.

00:07:40.056 --> 00:07:42.072
(umbrella smacking forehead)
- Oh!

00:07:42.072 --> 00:07:42.905
- Merry Christmas.

00:07:42.905 --> 00:07:43.738
- Henry, listen,

00:07:43.738 --> 00:07:45.320
I'm no legal brain trust.

00:07:45.320 --> 00:07:47.350
I don't know a habeas from a corpus.

00:07:47.350 --> 00:07:48.720
But I do know politics.

00:07:48.720 --> 00:07:50.010
That's my racket.

00:07:50.010 --> 00:07:51.420
I got ya elected, didn't I?

00:07:51.420 --> 00:07:53.090
And I'm gonna try to get ya reelected.

00:07:53.090 --> 00:07:54.240
- I know Charley.

00:07:54.240 --> 00:07:56.160
- Judges are elected in
a lot of jurisdictions.

00:07:56.160 --> 00:07:57.590
It's a big problem actually.

00:07:57.590 --> 00:08:00.236
- And I'm telling ya, get off this case.

00:08:00.236 --> 00:08:01.069
- Buy why?

00:08:01.069 --> 00:08:02.750
- Because you're a regular Pontius Pilate

00:08:02.750 --> 00:08:04.178
the minute you start, that's why.

00:08:04.178 --> 00:08:06.950
- Oh I don't believe it!

00:08:06.950 --> 00:08:08.460
I'm an honest man,

00:08:08.460 --> 00:08:10.140
and nobody's going to hold it against me

00:08:10.140 --> 00:08:12.289
for doing my duty as I see it.

00:08:12.289 --> 00:08:15.570
- This sort of does highlight
the problems that judges have

00:08:15.570 --> 00:08:16.570
in staying objective.

00:08:16.570 --> 00:08:18.890
There are a lot of political consequences

00:08:18.890 --> 00:08:21.350
associated with different outcomes,

00:08:21.350 --> 00:08:23.370
and it's really difficult
to stay objective

00:08:23.370 --> 00:08:24.739
in these circumstances.

00:08:24.739 --> 00:08:28.873
- Hey.
(courtroom chatter)

00:08:31.590 --> 00:08:33.260
- Taking a cigar into court.

00:08:33.260 --> 00:08:35.660
Boy, those were the bad old days.

00:08:35.660 --> 00:08:37.410
- [Bailiff] Hear ye, hear ye, hear ye.

00:08:37.410 --> 00:08:39.289
All person having business
with the special term

00:08:39.289 --> 00:08:41.130
part three of the Supreme Court

00:08:41.130 --> 00:08:43.240
held in and for the county of New York.

00:08:43.240 --> 00:08:46.870
Draw near and give your
attendance and ye shall be heard.

00:08:46.870 --> 00:08:49.310
- So what's happening here
is a motion by the state

00:08:49.310 --> 00:08:51.150
to involuntary commit someone,

00:08:51.150 --> 00:08:53.210
an elderly gentleman named Kris Kringle,

00:08:53.210 --> 00:08:55.230
to a mental institution.

00:08:55.230 --> 00:08:56.570
I actually had to look this up,

00:08:56.570 --> 00:08:59.010
because mental illness
law is not something

00:08:59.010 --> 00:09:01.720
that I practice with any frequency.

00:09:01.720 --> 00:09:03.980
And I thought it actually
might be a trope in movies

00:09:03.980 --> 00:09:05.210
that looks good but isn't true,

00:09:05.210 --> 00:09:07.180
like quicksand, for example.

00:09:07.180 --> 00:09:10.140
However, what I found is that
there is actually a procedure

00:09:10.140 --> 00:09:11.490
in New York law,

00:09:11.490 --> 00:09:13.720
to be able to involuntary commit someone

00:09:13.720 --> 00:09:15.580
for a mental illness,

00:09:15.580 --> 00:09:17.180
both in the present day,

00:09:17.180 --> 00:09:19.740
and in 1947 when this movie came out.

00:09:19.740 --> 00:09:22.470
And, in fact, what it requires
is that the state prove,

00:09:22.470 --> 00:09:24.100
by a preponderance of the evidence,

00:09:24.100 --> 00:09:25.520
that the individual in question

00:09:25.520 --> 00:09:27.000
suffers from a mental illness

00:09:27.000 --> 00:09:29.650
that requires immediate inpatient care,

00:09:29.650 --> 00:09:32.926
without which it's likely
to result in serious harm

00:09:32.926 --> 00:09:35.910
to that person or others around them.

00:09:35.910 --> 00:09:38.150
So, if those requirements are met,

00:09:38.150 --> 00:09:39.960
it is possible for the state of New York

00:09:39.960 --> 00:09:43.360
to involuntarily commit
someone to a mental institution

00:09:43.360 --> 00:09:44.610
for treatment.

00:09:44.610 --> 00:09:45.443
Now you know.

00:09:49.128 --> 00:09:50.610
Get out of the well, man!

00:09:50.610 --> 00:09:52.120
The bailiff will tackle you!

00:09:52.120 --> 00:09:53.329
- I said before you.

00:09:53.329 --> 00:09:54.555
Your honor, please,

00:09:54.555 --> 00:09:55.800
I should like to call the first witness.

00:09:55.800 --> 00:09:57.936
Mr. Kringle, will you take the stand?

00:09:57.936 --> 00:09:59.851
- His first witness is the defendant?

00:09:59.851 --> 00:10:02.250
- Mr. Kringle,

00:10:02.250 --> 00:10:05.010
you don't have to answer any
questions against your wishes,

00:10:05.010 --> 00:10:07.140
or even testify at all.

00:10:07.140 --> 00:10:08.840
- We have no objection your honor.

00:10:10.370 --> 00:10:13.270
- Oh, I'll be glad to answer
any question that I can.

00:10:13.270 --> 00:10:16.270
- So that was a huge strategic mistake.

00:10:16.270 --> 00:10:18.410
Kris Kringle here has
a fifth amendment right

00:10:18.410 --> 00:10:19.850
not to testify.

00:10:19.850 --> 00:10:21.740
And it's common for a
lot of people to think,

00:10:21.740 --> 00:10:23.800
well, if you don't have anything to hide,

00:10:23.800 --> 00:10:25.390
then you might as well take the stand.

00:10:25.390 --> 00:10:27.530
But that is the wrong way to look at it,

00:10:27.530 --> 00:10:29.800
because everyone has testimony

00:10:29.800 --> 00:10:31.560
that may come back to haunt them.

00:10:31.560 --> 00:10:32.790
And bad things can happen,

00:10:32.790 --> 00:10:34.220
based on your own testimony.

00:10:34.220 --> 00:10:35.851
So it is especially important

00:10:35.851 --> 00:10:38.410
where the state bears the burden of proof,

00:10:38.410 --> 00:10:40.600
not to give them ammunition.

00:10:40.600 --> 00:10:43.470
And here, we know that there
are skeletons in the closet,

00:10:43.470 --> 00:10:46.040
because Kris Kringle has
bashed someone over the head

00:10:46.040 --> 00:10:47.960
with the blunt end of an umbrella.

00:10:47.960 --> 00:10:49.860
He has committed assault.

00:10:49.860 --> 00:10:52.330
And, it's possible he
committed felony assault

00:10:52.330 --> 00:10:53.890
against the fake psychiatrist.

00:10:53.890 --> 00:10:56.540
And in a hearing over whether
you present an immediate

00:10:56.540 --> 00:10:58.870
danger to yourself or others,

00:10:58.870 --> 00:11:01.950
that kind of testimony is going
to be very, very important,

00:11:01.950 --> 00:11:03.945
and in fact, that testimony alone

00:11:03.945 --> 00:11:06.620
might be enough to meet
the state's burden here

00:11:06.620 --> 00:11:09.640
to show that he needs to
be committed involuntarily

00:11:09.640 --> 00:11:11.346
to a mental institution.

00:11:11.346 --> 00:11:13.133
- Where do you live?

00:11:13.133 --> 00:11:16.225
- That's what this hearing will decide.

00:11:16.225 --> 00:11:19.844
(courtroom laughing)

00:11:19.844 --> 00:11:22.387
(gavel banging)

00:11:22.387 --> 00:11:23.870
- A very sound answer, Mr. Kringle.

00:11:23.870 --> 00:11:26.050
- The judge should not
be opining at this point.

00:11:26.050 --> 00:11:28.540
- You believe that you're Santa Claus?

00:11:28.540 --> 00:11:30.255
- Of course.

00:11:30.255 --> 00:11:31.088
(chuckling)

00:11:31.088 --> 00:11:33.474
- But what does it mean to be Santa Claus?

00:11:33.474 --> 00:11:35.640
(upbeat tones)

00:11:35.640 --> 00:11:36.930
In a Cartesian way,

00:11:36.930 --> 00:11:39.610
the fact that he actually
thinks he is Santa Claus

00:11:39.610 --> 00:11:41.490
is a form of being Santa Claus.

00:11:41.490 --> 00:11:43.800
And, in fact, one who plays Santa Claus

00:11:43.800 --> 00:11:45.790
is a type of Santa Claus,

00:11:45.790 --> 00:11:48.934
perhaps a transitory or
temporary Santa Claus.

00:11:48.934 --> 00:11:51.660
I don't know that this state
has made it's burden here.

00:11:51.660 --> 00:11:53.250
I think it has a long way to go

00:11:53.250 --> 00:11:56.663
in arguing both the philosophical
and legal questions here.

00:11:59.810 --> 00:12:01.460
- State rests your honor.

00:12:01.460 --> 00:12:02.603
- Ooh.

00:12:03.800 --> 00:12:04.830
So at this point,

00:12:04.830 --> 00:12:06.580
what that means is that the state

00:12:06.580 --> 00:12:08.560
is not going to put on any more witnesses,

00:12:08.560 --> 00:12:11.450
and it's not going to put
forward any more evidence.

00:12:11.450 --> 00:12:13.750
The only testimony that has come out

00:12:13.750 --> 00:12:17.270
is the testimony that's come
by way of Kris Kringle himself.

00:12:17.270 --> 00:12:18.960
Which is crazy considering

00:12:18.960 --> 00:12:22.510
he could have put the fake
psychiatrist on the stand,

00:12:22.510 --> 00:12:25.480
to explain that he actually
was physically assaulted

00:12:25.480 --> 00:12:26.740
by Kris Kringle.

00:12:26.740 --> 00:12:29.000
That would've gone a long way
towards meeting his burden.

00:12:29.000 --> 00:12:31.360
All he is done is asked a few questions,

00:12:31.360 --> 00:12:33.107
which are ambiguous at best,

00:12:33.107 --> 00:12:37.100
to establish the mental state
of this individual person,

00:12:37.100 --> 00:12:38.960
and hasn't had any testimony

00:12:38.960 --> 00:12:41.780
that goes to whether
he is a physical danger

00:12:41.780 --> 00:12:43.300
to himself or others.

00:12:43.300 --> 00:12:44.660
So at this point,

00:12:44.660 --> 00:12:46.060
I don't think there's any argument

00:12:46.060 --> 00:12:48.040
that the state has not made its burden.

00:12:48.040 --> 00:12:49.650
I think that many be intentional.

00:12:49.650 --> 00:12:50.483
The prosecutor (chuckling)

00:12:50.483 --> 00:12:51.910
probably doesn't wanna ruin Christmas,

00:12:51.910 --> 00:12:54.090
the judge doesn't wanna ruin Christmas,

00:12:54.090 --> 00:12:57.067
and face the ire of his grandchildren.

00:12:57.067 --> 00:13:00.790
So I think that he is
intentionally tanking this case

00:13:00.790 --> 00:13:02.290
to not go forward.

00:13:02.290 --> 00:13:04.680
- I believe he was employed
to play Santa Claus,

00:13:04.680 --> 00:13:07.520
perhaps he didn't understand
the question correctly.

00:13:07.520 --> 00:13:10.097
- Oh, I understood the
question perfectly, your honor.

00:13:10.097 --> 00:13:11.080
(chuckling)

00:13:11.080 --> 00:13:12.410
- That doesn't actually
solve anything though.

00:13:12.410 --> 00:13:13.952
He can understand

00:13:13.952 --> 00:13:15.050
it and the question can
still mean different things.

00:13:15.050 --> 00:13:16.520
- No further questions at this time.

00:13:16.520 --> 00:13:18.675
- Yeah, there should definitely

00:13:18.675 --> 00:13:20.461
not be any cross examination.

00:13:20.461 --> 00:13:22.249
- Thank you.

00:13:22.249 --> 00:13:24.499
(laughing)

00:13:25.990 --> 00:13:27.030
In view of this statement,

00:13:27.030 --> 00:13:30.410
do you still wish to put
in a defense, young man?

00:13:30.410 --> 00:13:31.610
- I do, your honor.

00:13:31.610 --> 00:13:32.610
- Ooh.

00:13:32.610 --> 00:13:34.710
So what the judge is
hinting at there (chuckling)

00:13:34.710 --> 00:13:37.280
is that the defense should not go forward,

00:13:37.280 --> 00:13:39.760
because the state has not made its burden.

00:13:39.760 --> 00:13:42.740
Instead, what the defense
really should have done here

00:13:42.740 --> 00:13:44.750
is filed for a directed verdict

00:13:44.750 --> 00:13:47.868
or a motion for judgment
as a matter of law, a JMOL.

00:13:47.868 --> 00:13:49.490
What those motions do

00:13:50.361 --> 00:13:51.780
is they signal to the
court that the person

00:13:51.780 --> 00:13:53.490
or entity that has the burden,

00:13:53.490 --> 00:13:55.730
in this case the State of New York,

00:13:55.730 --> 00:13:57.450
has not met their burden.

00:13:57.450 --> 00:14:00.250
Taking the evidence in
a light most favorable

00:14:00.250 --> 00:14:01.500
to the moving party,

00:14:01.500 --> 00:14:03.030
it doesn't rise to the level

00:14:03.030 --> 00:14:04.510
of the thing that they have to prove.

00:14:04.510 --> 00:14:06.450
The state has the burden of proof.

00:14:06.450 --> 00:14:10.080
They have to prove that Kris
Kringle has a mental illness

00:14:10.080 --> 00:14:12.380
that is a danger to himself and others.

00:14:12.380 --> 00:14:14.330
They have not met their burden.

00:14:14.330 --> 00:14:17.070
It was a big mistake
by the self-proclaimed

00:14:17.070 --> 00:14:19.230
greatest lawyer in the world,

00:14:19.230 --> 00:14:21.630
to not stop this trial right here,

00:14:21.630 --> 00:14:24.515
because there's no reason
this trial should continue.

00:14:24.515 --> 00:14:27.359
- Anyone who thinks he's
Santa Claus is not sane!

00:14:27.359 --> 00:14:28.819
- Not necessarily.

00:14:28.819 --> 00:14:31.870
You believe yourself to be Judge Harper.

00:14:31.870 --> 00:14:33.760
Yet no one questions your sanity,

00:14:33.760 --> 00:14:35.640
because you are Judge Harper.

00:14:35.640 --> 00:14:37.621
- I know all about myself young man.

00:14:37.621 --> 00:14:38.753
(laughing)

00:14:38.753 --> 00:14:39.890
Mr. Kringle is the
subject of this hearing.

00:14:39.890 --> 00:14:41.398
- Yes, your honor.

00:14:41.398 --> 00:14:42.406
- All right, getting metaphysical.

00:14:42.406 --> 00:14:43.880
- And if he is the person
he believes himself to be,

00:14:43.880 --> 00:14:45.370
just as you are,

00:14:45.370 --> 00:14:46.990
then he's just as sane.

00:14:46.990 --> 00:14:47.949
- Granted, but he isn't.

00:14:47.949 --> 00:14:49.980
- Oh, but he is, your honor.

00:14:49.980 --> 00:14:50.910
- Is what?

00:14:50.910 --> 00:14:53.712
- I intend to prove that
Mr. Kringle is Santa Claus.

00:14:53.712 --> 00:14:54.904
(murmuring from courtroom)

00:14:54.904 --> 00:14:56.180
(laughing)

00:14:56.180 --> 00:14:58.240
- That's very, very amusing,

00:14:58.240 --> 00:15:00.530
but a terrible defense strategy.

00:15:00.530 --> 00:15:03.440
He has now taken on an affirmative burden,

00:15:03.440 --> 00:15:05.500
which the law doesn't put on him,

00:15:05.500 --> 00:15:08.280
and he has decided not to end this trial

00:15:08.280 --> 00:15:09.810
when it should have been ended,

00:15:09.810 --> 00:15:11.790
and decided to prove something

00:15:11.790 --> 00:15:13.695
that he doesn't actually have to prove.

00:15:13.695 --> 00:15:15.370
Not a good strategy,

00:15:15.370 --> 00:15:18.560
when you're sitting in a
winning position as a defense

00:15:18.560 --> 00:15:20.600
when the prosecution
hasn't made their case.

00:15:20.600 --> 00:15:22.800
- Go in and get mother's
scissors, will you?

00:15:24.644 --> 00:15:25.720
They're in the bedroom.

00:15:25.720 --> 00:15:26.863
That's a good boy.

00:15:28.760 --> 00:15:31.380
I don't want you to discuss
this case in front of him.

00:15:31.380 --> 00:15:32.980
It would break his heart.

00:15:32.980 --> 00:15:33.900
And while we on the subject,

00:15:33.900 --> 00:15:35.450
I agree with the reporters.

00:15:35.450 --> 00:15:37.140
Mr. Kringle seems to be a nice old man,

00:15:37.140 --> 00:15:39.420
and I don't see why you have
to keep persecuting him.

00:15:39.420 --> 00:15:41.650
Firstly, I am not persecuting him,

00:15:41.650 --> 00:15:43.220
I am prosecuting him.

00:15:43.220 --> 00:15:46.021
And secondly, I like the old man too!

00:15:46.021 --> 00:15:47.939
Wish I had never gotten into this.

00:15:47.939 --> 00:15:50.790
But it's too late now and there's
nothing I can do about it.

00:15:50.790 --> 00:15:51.940
It's up to the State of New York,

00:15:51.940 --> 00:15:54.870
and I'm just their duly
appointed legal representative.

00:15:54.870 --> 00:15:56.750
Kringle has been declared
a menace to society

00:15:56.750 --> 00:15:57.980
by competent doctors,

00:15:57.980 --> 00:15:59.970
and it's my duty to protect
the State of New York

00:15:59.970 --> 00:16:01.675
and see that he's put away.

00:16:01.675 --> 00:16:03.060
No matter what they may say about me,

00:16:03.060 --> 00:16:04.643
I've got to do it.

00:16:04.643 --> 00:16:07.660
- I think the prosecutor does
have an ethical obligation

00:16:07.660 --> 00:16:10.610
to go forward with his
case as a zealous advocate

00:16:10.610 --> 00:16:12.720
for the State of New York,

00:16:12.720 --> 00:16:13.553
but on the other hand,

00:16:13.553 --> 00:16:16.310
he also has the ability to
dismiss this case entirely,

00:16:16.310 --> 00:16:18.387
and to drop the case.

00:16:18.387 --> 00:16:20.860
I don't think the DA has to proceed.

00:16:20.860 --> 00:16:22.900
So that's an interesting ethical quandary.

00:16:22.900 --> 00:16:24.670
I'm not sure which way that comes out

00:16:24.670 --> 00:16:26.230
in favor of the prosecutor here.

00:16:26.230 --> 00:16:29.338
I think he probably could
drop it if he wanted to.

00:16:29.338 --> 00:16:32.505
(courtroom muttering)

00:16:33.490 --> 00:16:35.190
- Psychologist.

00:16:35.190 --> 00:16:36.610
Where'd you graduate from,

00:16:36.610 --> 00:16:37.810
a correspondence school?

00:16:39.350 --> 00:16:40.880
You're fired.

00:16:40.880 --> 00:16:42.425
- Ooh.

00:16:42.425 --> 00:16:43.872
And now he has a claim for retaliation.

00:16:43.872 --> 00:16:46.310
- Mr. Marrah seems to have
appointed himself to judge here,

00:16:46.310 --> 00:16:47.143
your honor.

00:16:48.193 --> 00:16:49.580
He's now ruling on what
testimony I may introduce.

00:16:49.580 --> 00:16:51.161
- Your honor,

00:16:51.161 --> 00:16:52.080
we request an immediate
ruling from this court.

00:16:52.080 --> 00:16:55.280
Is there, or is there not, a Santa Claus?

00:16:55.280 --> 00:16:57.170
- So I actually think that this court

00:16:57.170 --> 00:16:59.320
may be constitutionally prohibited

00:16:59.320 --> 00:17:01.410
from weighing in on that question.

00:17:01.410 --> 00:17:04.970
Because remember, Santa
Claus is a religious figure,

00:17:04.970 --> 00:17:06.031
Saint Nicholas.

00:17:06.031 --> 00:17:08.270
And under the first amendment,

00:17:08.270 --> 00:17:11.610
courts cannot weight in
on religious decisions.

00:17:11.610 --> 00:17:13.900
Under the United States
Supreme Court Jurisprudence,

00:17:13.900 --> 00:17:16.050
specifically the US versus. Ballard case

00:17:16.050 --> 00:17:17.490
that came out in 1944,

00:17:17.490 --> 00:17:20.070
just three years before this movie did,

00:17:20.070 --> 00:17:22.130
courts are prohibited
under the first amendment

00:17:22.130 --> 00:17:23.920
from deciding religious questions.

00:17:23.920 --> 00:17:27.610
In other words, they cannot
decide the truth or falsity

00:17:27.610 --> 00:17:30.070
of any particular religious tenent.

00:17:30.070 --> 00:17:31.680
- And what about the Salvation Army?

00:17:31.680 --> 00:17:34.530
Why, they've got a Santa
Claus on every corner,

00:17:34.530 --> 00:17:36.570
and they take in a fortune.

00:17:36.570 --> 00:17:38.040
But you go ahead Henry,

00:17:38.040 --> 00:17:39.860
you do it your way.

00:17:39.860 --> 00:17:42.120
You go on back in there and tell 'em

00:17:42.120 --> 00:17:44.370
that your rule there's no Santy Claus.

00:17:44.370 --> 00:17:45.213
Go on!

00:17:46.770 --> 00:17:48.657
But if you do, remember this:

00:17:48.657 --> 00:17:50.790
you can count on getting just two votes.

00:17:50.790 --> 00:17:54.200
Your own, and that district
attorney's out there.

00:17:54.200 --> 00:17:56.360
- The judge really shouldn't
be in this situation at all.

00:17:56.360 --> 00:17:59.340
Because most judges ascribe
to the judicial canon

00:17:59.340 --> 00:18:02.460
that the court should only
decide the minimum number

00:18:02.460 --> 00:18:04.611
of questions that are absolutely necessary

00:18:04.611 --> 00:18:07.140
for the resolution of the case at hand.

00:18:07.140 --> 00:18:10.650
And here, the judge doesn't
actually have to weigh in

00:18:10.650 --> 00:18:11.710
on this question.

00:18:11.710 --> 00:18:13.470
The court, really, should just say

00:18:13.470 --> 00:18:15.670
that the prosection
has not met its burden,

00:18:15.670 --> 00:18:17.170
not weigh in on the larger question

00:18:17.170 --> 00:18:19.710
of whether there is a Santa Claus or not.

00:18:19.710 --> 00:18:22.163
- Can he produce any evidence
to support his views?

00:18:22.163 --> 00:18:24.146
- If, your honor, please, I can.

00:18:24.146 --> 00:18:27.050
Will Thomas Marrah please take the stand.

00:18:27.050 --> 00:18:27.940
- Who, me?

00:18:27.940 --> 00:18:32.483
- Thomas Marrah junior.
(courtroom muttering)

00:18:37.610 --> 00:18:39.407
- They gave him a subpoena (laughing).

00:18:40.900 --> 00:18:42.640
Well, if you're going to
call the prosecutor's son,

00:18:42.640 --> 00:18:44.570
you have to subpoena them.

00:18:44.570 --> 00:18:46.550
- Do you believe in Santa Claus, Tommy?

00:18:46.550 --> 00:18:48.033
- Sure I do.

00:18:48.033 --> 00:18:52.540
He gave me a brand new
flexible flyer sled last year.

00:18:52.540 --> 00:18:53.817
And this year--

00:18:53.817 --> 00:18:55.670
- And what does he look like?

00:18:55.670 --> 00:18:58.630
- There is he, sitting there!
(courtroom laughing)

00:18:58.630 --> 00:18:59.880
- Your honor, I protest!

00:18:59.880 --> 00:19:01.290
- He has a positive ID.

00:19:01.290 --> 00:19:02.743
- Overruled.

00:19:02.743 --> 00:19:04.239
- Overruled.

00:19:04.239 --> 00:19:06.284
- Tell me, Tommy,

00:19:06.284 --> 00:19:09.610
why are you so sure this is Santa Claus?

00:19:09.610 --> 00:19:12.274
- Because my daddy told me so.

00:19:12.274 --> 00:19:13.107
(courtroom laughing)

00:19:13.107 --> 00:19:14.705
Didn't you daddy?

00:19:14.705 --> 00:19:15.702
- Hearsay.

00:19:15.702 --> 00:19:18.050
- And you believe your
daddy, don't you Tommy?

00:19:18.050 --> 00:19:19.651
- Should be objecting here.

00:19:19.651 --> 00:19:21.460
Also, he should be fighting like hell

00:19:21.460 --> 00:19:24.250
to prevent his son from testifying here.

00:19:24.250 --> 00:19:25.260
That's totally improper.

00:19:25.260 --> 00:19:26.540
But, adorable.

00:19:26.540 --> 00:19:28.120
- He's a very honest man.

00:19:28.120 --> 00:19:29.590
- 'Course he is.

00:19:29.590 --> 00:19:32.293
My daddy wouldn't tell me
anything that wasn't so.

00:19:33.302 --> 00:19:34.729
Would you daddy?

00:19:34.729 --> 00:19:36.189
(courtroom laughing)

00:19:36.189 --> 00:19:37.993
- Thank you Tommy.

00:19:40.250 --> 00:19:41.493
- No cross-examination?

00:19:42.700 --> 00:19:44.261
- Goodbye daddy.

00:19:44.261 --> 00:19:46.511
(laughing)

00:19:53.710 --> 00:19:55.020
- Your honor, the State of New York

00:19:55.020 --> 00:19:57.325
concedes the existence of Santa Claus.

00:19:57.325 --> 00:19:59.400
(laughing)

00:19:59.400 --> 00:20:02.120
- Oh, that may be an ethical
issue for him right there.

00:20:02.120 --> 00:20:03.450
But he shouldn't have conceded,

00:20:03.450 --> 00:20:05.550
he should have just dropped the case.

00:20:05.550 --> 00:20:07.245
There wouldn't have been
any precedential value

00:20:07.245 --> 00:20:09.470
in this particular trial,

00:20:09.470 --> 00:20:11.650
if the DA just gave up prosecuting it

00:20:11.650 --> 00:20:14.600
and withdrew the motion to
commit the defendant here.

00:20:14.600 --> 00:20:16.642
This is a problem of their own creation.

00:20:16.642 --> 00:20:20.161
- I therefore request
that Mr. Gailey now submit

00:20:20.161 --> 00:20:21.900
authoritative proof,

00:20:21.900 --> 00:20:25.350
that Mr. Kringle is the
one and only Santa Claus.

00:20:25.350 --> 00:20:26.360
- That's not a legal standard.

00:20:26.360 --> 00:20:28.660
There's no such thing
as authoritative proof.

00:20:28.660 --> 00:20:31.500
There is proof, and you
have to meet your burden,

00:20:31.500 --> 00:20:32.373
generally speaking here,

00:20:32.373 --> 00:20:34.900
proof by preponderance of the evidence.

00:20:34.900 --> 00:20:37.600
But there's no such thing as
quote, authoritative proof.

00:20:37.600 --> 00:20:40.080
- Your honor, I'm sure we're all gratified

00:20:40.080 --> 00:20:42.680
to know that the post office
department is doing so nicely,

00:20:42.680 --> 00:20:45.240
but it hardly has any
bearing on this case.

00:20:45.240 --> 00:20:46.620
- It has a great deal, your honor,

00:20:46.620 --> 00:20:48.470
if I may be allowed to proceed.

00:20:48.470 --> 00:20:50.023
- By all means, Mr. Gailey.

00:20:51.980 --> 00:20:52.813
- Your honor,

00:20:52.813 --> 00:20:54.440
the figures I have just quoted

00:20:54.440 --> 00:20:57.210
indicate an efficiently run organization.

00:20:57.210 --> 00:21:00.430
Furthermore, the United States
postal laws and regulations

00:21:00.430 --> 00:21:03.770
make it a criminal offense
to wailfully misdirect mail,

00:21:03.770 --> 00:21:06.720
or intentionally deliver
it to the wrong party.

00:21:06.720 --> 00:21:09.140
- So there is something
called the supremacy clause

00:21:09.140 --> 00:21:10.350
in the US constitution,

00:21:10.350 --> 00:21:14.040
that requires that where the
federal government has acted

00:21:14.040 --> 00:21:16.960
that preempts state law
contradictory action

00:21:16.960 --> 00:21:18.730
on that particular point.

00:21:18.730 --> 00:21:22.000
But, on the other hand, it
does create a precedence.

00:21:22.000 --> 00:21:25.868
State laws and courts rely on regulations

00:21:25.868 --> 00:21:27.470
from the federal government,

00:21:27.470 --> 00:21:29.330
not for their preclusive effect,

00:21:29.330 --> 00:21:32.230
but often for their authority
in very different cases.

00:21:32.230 --> 00:21:34.700
For example, if a state
court is deciding a matter

00:21:34.700 --> 00:21:36.290
or first impression,

00:21:36.290 --> 00:21:39.230
it will often rely on
federal court decisions,

00:21:39.230 --> 00:21:42.310
or administrative decisions
of the federal government,

00:21:42.310 --> 00:21:43.590
in crafting its opinion,

00:21:43.590 --> 00:21:46.720
because while it's not
bound by those decisions,

00:21:46.720 --> 00:21:50.496
it can use those opinions
and decisions and regulations

00:21:50.496 --> 00:21:52.670
as persuasive authority.

00:21:52.670 --> 00:21:55.010
If the federal government
has decided something,

00:21:55.010 --> 00:21:57.160
it lends a great deal
of credence to the idea

00:21:57.160 --> 00:21:59.760
that that is what should actually happen

00:21:59.760 --> 00:22:01.110
in a matter of first impression

00:22:01.110 --> 00:22:02.250
before the state court.

00:22:02.250 --> 00:22:03.660
So what's happening here,

00:22:03.660 --> 00:22:05.746
relying on the post office,

00:22:05.746 --> 00:22:09.040
presumably the administrative
decision of the post office,

00:22:09.040 --> 00:22:10.320
has a ring of truth to it.

00:22:10.320 --> 00:22:12.401
State court judges to that all the time.

00:22:12.401 --> 00:22:14.960
- I have further exhibits your honor,

00:22:14.960 --> 00:22:16.470
but I hesitate to produce them.

00:22:16.470 --> 00:22:18.533
- Oh, I'm sure we'll be
very happy to see them.

00:22:18.533 --> 00:22:20.220
- Yes, yes, yes.

00:22:20.220 --> 00:22:21.650
Produce them Mr. Gailey.

00:22:21.650 --> 00:22:23.190
Put them here on my desk.

00:22:23.190 --> 00:22:24.786
- But your honor--

00:22:24.786 --> 00:22:26.599
- Put them here on the desk.

00:22:26.599 --> 00:22:27.432
Put them.

00:22:28.840 --> 00:22:30.390
- [Mr. Gailey] Yes, your honor.

00:22:38.478 --> 00:22:41.645
(courtroom muttering)

00:22:43.770 --> 00:22:46.516
- That's what we call the
overwhelming weight of authority.

00:22:46.516 --> 00:22:48.176
(comedic drums)

00:22:48.176 --> 00:22:50.537
(dog whimpering)

00:22:50.537 --> 00:22:53.120
(upbeat music)

00:22:54.580 --> 00:22:57.290
All right, now it's time to
give this holiday classic

00:22:57.290 --> 00:22:59.131
a grade for legal realism.

00:22:59.131 --> 00:23:01.180
(gavel banging)

00:23:01.180 --> 00:23:03.230
It's been a long time
since I've seen this movie,

00:23:03.230 --> 00:23:05.806
and I'm delighted to see
all the legal accuracies.

00:23:05.806 --> 00:23:08.440
The hearing, regarding mental commitment

00:23:08.440 --> 00:23:12.140
is both based in law and
largely factually correct.

00:23:12.140 --> 00:23:15.350
The legal arguments are
inventive, but somewhat realistic.

00:23:15.350 --> 00:23:17.330
However, on the other side,
they gloss over the fact

00:23:17.330 --> 00:23:19.330
that Santa Claus committed assault,

00:23:19.330 --> 00:23:21.430
that he waived his fifth amendment rights,

00:23:21.430 --> 00:23:24.431
and kinda screwed up some of
the constitutional arguments.

00:23:24.431 --> 00:23:28.100
But this is a clear holiday classic,

00:23:28.100 --> 00:23:31.180
so I'm going to give this
wonderful holiday movie

00:23:31.180 --> 00:23:32.340
three candy canes.

00:23:32.340 --> 00:23:34.500
It's so wonderful, I don't
see how I can give it

00:23:34.500 --> 00:23:35.890
anything less than that.

00:23:35.890 --> 00:23:37.210
And on the question

00:23:37.210 --> 00:23:40.100
of whether there really
is a Santa Claus or not.

00:23:40.100 --> 00:23:42.260
Well, as an officer of the court,

00:23:42.260 --> 00:23:44.420
I am bound by Supreme Court precedence,

00:23:44.420 --> 00:23:46.428
and I can't lose my impartiality.

00:23:46.428 --> 00:23:47.670
But I will say this,

00:23:47.670 --> 00:23:49.010
if the real Santa Claus is out there,

00:23:49.010 --> 00:23:50.916
and you're looking for the
best attorney in the world,

00:23:50.916 --> 00:23:52.702
I will represent you pro bono.

00:23:52.702 --> 00:23:55.490
So happy holidays legal eagles.

00:23:55.490 --> 00:23:56.810
And until next time,

00:23:56.810 --> 00:23:58.563
I'll see you in court.

